<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laurentian Codex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1377">1377</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Laurentian Chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laurentian Codex is a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copied in 1377 by the monk Lavrenty for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-of-suzdal">Dmitry Konstantinovich of Suzdal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>. It contains the oldest surviving copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, followed by a continuation based on the chronicles of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> to 1305. It is held in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in St Petersburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laurentian-codex.jpg" alt="Laurentian Codex" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolay Omonov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurentian_Codex_07,_Calling_of_varangians.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Laurentian Codex, or Laurentian Letopis, is a collection of chronicles that includes the oldest extant version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> and its continuations, mostly relating the events of the north-eastern Rus' principalities of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. It was copied in 1377 by Laurentius, a monk from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>. Its record of events from 1284 to 1305 goes back to a lost codex compiled in 1305 for Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail of Tver</a>. Laurentius re-edited the presentation of Yuri Vsevolodovich, the founder of Nizhny Novgorod, from a positive one into a negative one and partly rehabilitated the role of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. Vasily Komarovich studied the traces of such changes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in 1976 and proposed a hypothesis on how Laurentius's version differed from the lost Tver one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The codex brings together several codices of the Vladimir chronicles. Its text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> covers events from 852 to the 1110s, and its text of the Suzdalian Chronicle covers 1111 to 1305. The Primary Chronicle appears here in its second edition, the one revised in 1116 by Sylvester, hegumen of the St Michael Monastery at Vydubychi, in the reign of Prince Vladimir Monomakh. The codex is the only source for the autobiographical text known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instruction-of-vladimir-monomakh">Instruction of Vladimir Monomakh</a>.</p><p>The first part, up to folio 40 verso, was written by an unknown scribe commissioned by Andrew Bogolyubsky and finished in 1177 after the prince's assassination. A chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a> continued to 1193, and a third part glorifying Vsevolod was composed in 1212 by his son Yuri Vsevolodovich. The Vladimir chronicles borrowed from sources of southern Rus', especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a>, which the Vladimir princes regarded as part of their patrimony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, 173 leaves survive and 12 are lost. Six leaves are missing between folios 9 and 10, covering the years 898 to 921, five after folio 169, for 1263 to 1283, and one after folio 170, for 1288 to 1293. The number of lost leaves is estimated from parallel texts in nearby chronicles. One hypothesis explains the last gap as an omission in the model copy rather than a lost leaf. The compilation was done in great haste, and a second scribe helped Laurentius.</p><p>Jaroslaw Pelenski compared the Suzdalian Chronicle with the Kievan Chronicle in the Hypatian Codex in 1988. The Kievan text is ambivalent or critical towards the reign of Andrew Bogolyubsky, whereas the Suzdalian text is positive towards him. Count Musin-Pushkin acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in 1792 and gave it to the Russian National Library in St Petersburg. The standard edition appeared in 1926 to 1928, and an English translation of the Laurentian text of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> by Samuel Hazzard Cross and Olgerd Sherbowitz-Wetzor in 1953.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Laurentian Codex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vladimir chronicle tradition survives in the <b>Laurentian Codex</b>, copied in 1377, which also preserves the oldest copy of the Primary Chronicle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
